    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All green \u2014 the full suite (2 tests) passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nChanged owner deletion from a hard delete to a soft delete, keeping each concern in its natural home:\n\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `deleted` field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`) initialized to `Boolean.FALSE`, plus getter/setter. The field initializer is what makes a newly created owner serialize `deleted: false` (the insert writes `false` rather than `NULL`).\n- **`rest/function/owner/DeleteOwner.java`** \u2014 now flags the loaded owner `deleted=true` and `save`s it (was `repository.delete(owner)`). The DELETE pipeline is otherwise unchanged, so it still responds `204` (2xx) and the row is retained; `GET /api/owners/{id}` still loads it by id and returns `deleted: true`.\n- **`rest/function/owner/EnsureUniqueIdentity.java`** (the blocking identity check) and **`AssignPossibleDuplicate.java`** (the soft duplicate check) \u2014 each now skips owners flagged deleted while scanning existing owners, so a normally-blocking household collision is allowed when the only match has been soft-deleted.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps `Owner.getDeleted()` \u2192 `OwnerDto.deleted`.\n- **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** and **`db/h2/schema.sql`** (the test and main profiles) \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` to the `owners` table.\n\nI scoped the exclusion precisely to the create endpoint's duplicate/identity checks (as the spec requires), leaving `findById` \u2014 and therefore GET and the delete flow \u2014 returning soft-deleted owners untouched. Inactive `jpa`/`jdbc` repository profiles were left alone since they aren't wired in for the app or tests.",
